    "explanation": "Carbamazepine, a sodium-channel-blocking antiepileptic, is used off-label in post-traumatic brain injury agitation primarily for its mood-stabilizing properties, extrapolated from its established efficacy in bipolar disorder and other conditions marked by affective lability and irritability. Clinical reviews of pharmacologic management of TBI-related behavioral disturbance describe carbamazepine and valproate as \"mood stabilizers\" recommended as first-line agents specifically for agitation, aggressiveness, anger, and irritability, particularly when accompanied by mood swings -- not because of anticonvulsant, sedative, or anxiolytic action in this context, even though the drug also happens to have those other properties for different indications. This distinguishes carbamazepine's therapeutic rationale in agitated TBI patients (mood/behavior stabilization) from its use in epilepsy (seizure control) or from truly sedating or anxiolytic agents.\nReference: A Clinical Review of Mood Stabilizers in Traumatic Brain Injury, Current Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Reports, Springer, https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40141-024-00467-1 -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Mysiw WJ, Sandel ME. Arch Phys Med Rehabil 1997;78:2135) is not available to the user.",
