    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 51, Neurogenic Bladder and Bowel, Table 51.3, 'Treatment Options for Voiding Disorders') lists, for 'Incontinence Caused by the Bladder' (i.e., detrusor overactivity, as would be expected with a normal sphincter EMG in this poststroke patient), pharmacologic treatment with 'oral anticholinergics, antispasmodics, tricyclic antidepressants...' Anticholinergic medications are cholinergic antagonists that block muscarinic receptors on the detrusor, reducing involuntary bladder contractions; alpha-adrenergic antagonists and beta-adrenergic antagonists act on outlet/sphincter mechanisms rather than bladder overactivity, and cholinergic agonists would worsen, not treat, detrusor overactivity.\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 51, p.1358 (page number confirmed matching the original ABPMR citation exactly). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 5th ed. 2010, page 1358.",
