    "option_d": "Magnetic resonance arteriogram",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 23, stroke chapter, thrombolysis section) describes the standard acute stroke pathway as requiring 'immediate clinical evaluation, urgent CT scan, and infusion as soon as possible within the therapeutic window,' since CT rapidly distinguishes ischemic from hemorrhagic stroke and determines eligibility for time-sensitive interventions such as thrombolysis. In a postoperative patient with sudden aphasia and hemiparesis, noncontrast CT is obtained emergently before any other study because it is fast, widely available, and essential to rule out hemorrhage before further management (e.g., anticoagulation) is considered; arterial blood gases, EEG, and MR angiography do not provide this immediate, actionable information.\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 23, p.558 (page differs from the original ABPMR citation of p.1661, 4th ed., which does not correspond to this topic in this 5th-ed. PDF). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 4th ed. 2005, page 1661.",
