    "id": "moc100-25",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "Which electrodiagnostic technique is most useful in diagnosing a radiculopathy?",
    "option_a": "Nerve conduction study",
    "option_b": "Late responses",
    "option_c": "Needle electromyography",
    "option_d": "Repetitive nerve stimulation",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "Because a radiculopathy is a preganglionic lesion, routine sensory nerve conduction studies remain normal (the dorsal root ganglion and distal sensory axon are intact), so NCS alone cannot establish the diagnosis. Needle EMG, however, can directly sample the myotomal muscles supplied by a given nerve root -- including paraspinal muscles -- and detect denervation (fibrillations, positive sharp waves) or chronic reinnervation changes in a pattern that spans multiple peripheral nerves but a single root, which is the physiologic hallmark of radiculopathy. This is why needle EMG is described as having excellent specificity and is regarded as the most specific and useful diagnostic technique for confirming radiculopathy, while late responses (F-waves, H-reflex) and repetitive stimulation are used for other purposes (demyelinating polyneuropathy screening and neuromuscular junction disorders, respectively) and lack the localizing myotomal resolution of needle EMG.\nReference: Electrodiagnostic Evaluation of Cervical Radiculopathy, StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563152/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Wilbourn AJ, Aminoff MJ. AAEE Minimonograph #32. Muscle Nerve 1998;21:1612) is not available to the user.",
    "image": null,
    "video_link": ""
