    "id": "moc100-27",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "Chronic radicular pain in patients with a spinal cord injury (SCI) is most effectively treated with",
    "option_a": "muscle relaxants",
    "option_b": "narcotic analgesics",
    "option_c": "anticonvulsants",
    "option_d": "nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 49, 'Treatment of the Patient with Chronic Pain,' Table 49.9 and accompanying text) classifies at-level nerve root compression pain after SCI as neuropathic and notes that neuropathic and radicular pain syndromes are managed with neuropathic pain-relieving medications, specifically citing 'anticonvulsants (such as gabapentin, phenytoin, and carbamazepine)' as treatment for these radicular/neuropathic pain states. Muscle relaxants, NSAIDs, and narcotic analgesics are directed primarily at nociceptive musculoskeletal pain (per the same table's classification) and are generally less effective for the neuropathic, radicular component of SCI-related pain.\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 49, p.1306-1307 (page differs from the original ABPMR citation of p.523, 4th ed., which does not correspond to this topic in this 5th-ed. PDF). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 4th ed. 2005, page 523.",
