    "explanation": "The elbow flexor group targeted by botulinum toxin injection for post-stroke spasticity consists of the biceps brachii, brachialis, and brachioradialis -- the three principal muscles that flex the elbow -- and clinical studies of muscle-specific BoNT-A injection strategies for elbow flexor spasticity explicitly compare regimens targeting biceps brachii versus brachialis-plus-brachioradialis combinations, confirming brachioradialis's established role as a primary elbow flexor spasticity target alongside biceps and brachialis. Pectoralis major, teres major, and coracobrachialis primarily act at the shoulder (adduction/internal rotation or flexion) rather than as prime elbow flexors, so they are not standard targets for reducing elbow flexion hypertonicity, even though they may be injected for shoulder-related spasticity patterns.\nReference: Botulinum Toxin A for Elbow Flexor Spasticity: A Non-Randomized Observational Study of Muscle-Specific Injection Strategies, PMC, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC12155619/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Bell KR, Brander VA et al. Phys Med Rehabil Clin N Am. 2003, pp.828-829) is not available to the user.",
