    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 59, 'Aging and Rehabilitation,' sleep section) advises that 'acute insomnia (present for <1 month)...is treated with support and short-term, intermittent medication' and that hypnotics should be used judiciously in older patients given heightened sensitivity to sedating drugs. Standard geriatric prescribing teaching (consistent with this chapter's broader caution about anticholinergic and long-acting benzodiazepine effects in the elderly) favors a short-acting non-benzodiazepine hypnotic such as zolpidem for short-term insomnia over amitriptyline or diphenhydramine (both strongly anticholinergic, risking confusion and falls) or diazepam (long-acting benzodiazepine, risking daytime sedation and falls).\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 59, p.1572 (page differs slightly from the original ABPMR citation of p.1571, which in this PDF discusses dementia/incontinence rather than insomnia specifically; the surrounding aging chapter nonetheless supports the reasoning). Note: the exact statement naming zolpidem was not located verbatim via keyword search; explanation reflects standard PM&R/geriatric teaching on this topic combined with the located passage on management of acute insomnia in the elderly. Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 5th ed. 2010, page 1571.",
