    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "The most sensitive method available for early diagnosis of avascular necrosis of the hip is",
    "option_a": "magnetic resonance imaging",
    "option_b": "radionuclide bone scan",
    "option_c": "computed tomography scan",
    "option_d": "x-ray",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "MRI directly visualizes early bone marrow edema and the ischemic marrow changes that precede any structural collapse of the femoral head, giving it a reported sensitivity of over 99% for avascular necrosis (AVN) of the hip -- comparative imaging studies found MRI superior to both radionuclide bone scanning and CT across an entire case pool, with the difference exceeding 2 standard errors relative to bone scan. Plain radiographs remain normal in early-stage AVN (reported sensitivity as low as ~41%) because they can only detect later structural changes such as subchondral collapse (the crescent sign) or sclerosis, and CT, while better than x-ray, is still less sensitive than MRI for the earliest marrow-level ischemic changes. This makes MRI the imaging modality of choice for detecting AVN before irreversible collapse occurs, when joint-preserving treatment is still possible.\nReference: The Application of Magnetic Resonance Imaging in the Early and Accurate Diagnosis of Hip Joint Avascular Necrosis, PMC, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11458061/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (DeLee JC et al. DeLee & Drez's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine, 3rd ed. 2010, p.984) is not available to the user.",
