    "id": "moc100-42",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "Which provocative test evaluates both the hip and sacroiliac joints?",
    "option_a": "Straight leg raise (or Lasègue)",
    "option_b": "Patrick (or FABER)",
    "option_c": "Trendelenburg",
    "option_d": "Thomas, with resistance applied",
    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "The Patrick, or FABER (Flexion, ABduction, External Rotation), test places the tested leg into a 'figure-four' position with the foot resting on the opposite knee, a maneuver that stresses both the hip joint (via flexion/abduction/external rotation of the femur) and the sacroiliac joint (via the torsional force transmitted through the pelvis as the pelvis is stabilized and the hip is pressed toward the table). Pain localized anteriorly in the groin/hip on the tested side suggests intra-articular hip pathology, whereas pain posteriorly near the SI joint (often on the contralateral side) suggests sacroiliac joint dysfunction, which is why the test is used to differentiate -- and screen for -- pathology at either joint. The straight leg raise mainly stresses the lumbosacral nerve roots/sciatic nerve, Trendelenburg assesses hip abductor (gluteus medius) strength and stability, and the resisted Thomas test primarily evaluates hip flexor tightness/strength, none of which specifically assess the SI joint the way FABER does.\nReference: Patrick's test, Wikipedia (summarizing the original orthopedic literature on the Patrick/FABER maneuver), https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick's_test -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Boden, Weisel. Low Back Pain. 1995, p.91) is not available to the user.",
