    "question_text": "What is the most common cause of lumbar stenosis?",
    "option_a": "Disk herniation",
    "option_b": "Vertebral compression fractures",
    "option_c": "Degenerative disk disease",
    "option_d": "Spondylolysis/spondylolisthesis",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "Acquired (degenerative) lumbar spinal stenosis is by far the most common form seen clinically, and per StatPearls and Merck Manual reviews it results from age-related degenerative changes -- intervertebral disc degeneration/bulging, facet joint hypertrophy, and ligamentum flavum thickening -- that progressively narrow the central canal and neural foramina. Disk herniation, compression fractures, and spondylolisthesis can each contribute to canal narrowing, but they are less common overall causes than the cumulative degenerative cascade centered on disc disease and facet arthropathy. This is why degenerative disk disease, as part of the broader osteoarthritic/degenerative process, is identified as the leading etiology of lumbar stenosis in older adults.\nReference: StatPearls, \"Lumbar Spinal Stenosis,\" NCBI Bookshelf, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK531493/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Szpalski, M., Gunzburg R., Lumbar spinal stenosis in the elderly: an overview. The Aging Spine. 2005, page 88) is not available to the user.",
