    "explanation": "Osgood-Schlatter disease is a clinical diagnosis, but when imaging is obtained it is used mainly to confirm the clinical impression and exclude other causes of anterior knee pain in an adolescent. Per a Medscape/Radsource review, the tibial tuberosity lesion -- soft-tissue swelling, ossicle formation, and fragmentation -- is best demonstrated on the lateral knee radiograph, ideally with the knee in slight internal rotation, making it the appropriate first-line imaging study. MRI and CT are reserved for atypical presentations or when other intra-articular pathology is suspected, and a skyline (patellofemoral) view is aimed at the patellofemoral joint rather than the tibial tuberosity, so it is not the best initial choice here.\nReference: Osgood-Schlatter Disease Imaging, Medscape/Radsource review, https://radsource.us/osgood-schlatter-disease/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (DeLee JC et al. DeLee & Drez's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine: Principles and Practice. 3rd ed. 2010, page 1528) is not available to the user.",
