    "option_c": "Long thoracic",
    "option_d": "Thoracodorsal",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "The axillary nerve's course through the quadrangular space and its close relationship to the inferior glenohumeral capsule and surgical neck of the humerus make it the nerve most vulnerable to traction injury during anterior shoulder dislocation. StatPearls notes it is injured in roughly 5% (and up to higher rates in older patients or delayed reductions) of anterior dislocations, more often than the radial, long thoracic, or thoracodorsal nerves, which are anatomically more remote from the glenohumeral joint. Risk increases with patient age, degree of trauma, and duration the joint remains dislocated before reduction. This anatomic vulnerability is why axillary nerve palsy (deltoid weakness, lateral shoulder sensory loss) is routinely checked before and after reduction.\nReference: StatPearls, \"Axillary Nerve Injury,\" NCBI Bookshelf, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539895/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (DeLee JC et al. DeLee & Drez's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine: Principles and Practice. 3rd ed. 2010, page 933) is not available to the user.",
