    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A 28-year-old man develops insidious onset of chronic low back pain with morning stiffness. A diagnosis of ankylosing spondylitis requires",
    "option_a": "radiographic demonstration of sacroiliac joint abnormality",
    "option_b": "a positive HLA-B27 test",
    "option_c": "ophthalmologic confirmation of acute anterior uveitis",
    "option_d": "an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "Under the modified New York criteria, definite ankylosing spondylitis requires the radiologic criterion of sacroiliitis (grade >=2 bilaterally, or grade 3-4 unilaterally) plus at least one clinical criterion (inflammatory back pain, limited lumbar motion, or reduced chest expansion). Radiographic sacroiliitis has historically been the required, non-negotiable component -- described as the 'sine qua non' of the diagnosis -- without which classic AS could not be diagnosed by these criteria. HLA-B27 positivity, elevated ESR, and uveitis are all supportive/associated findings that increase suspicion for spondyloarthropathy but are not themselves required for the diagnosis of AS. This distinguishes AS from the newer 'non-radiographic axial spondyloarthritis' category, which uses HLA-B27 or MRI findings instead.\nReference: PMC review, 'The Nonradiographic Axial Spondyloarthritis, the Radiographic Axial Spondyloarthritis, and Ankylosing Spondylitis,' https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5438843/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited textbook (Klippel JH, ed. Primer on the Rheumatic Diseases. 13th ed. 2008, page 194) is not available to the user.",
