    "explanation": "Superficial heat modalities (hot packs, paraffin, warm hydrotherapy) rely on the patient's ability to sense escalating warmth and give timely feedback so the clinician can prevent thermal injury. A comparative review of contraindication guidelines found strong, consistent agreement across sources that impaired sensation/sensory deficit is a contraindication to superficial heat, because a patient who cannot discriminate mild from intense warmth is at high risk of an unrecognized burn. Joint replacement hardware and superficial thrombophlebitis are more commonly cited relative to deep heating modalities (e.g., ultrasound, diathermy) rather than superficial heat specifically, and an isolated hematoma is not a classic listed contraindication to superficial heat in the same way sensory loss is.\nReference: 'Contraindications for superficial heat and therapeutic ultrasound: Do sources agree?' Archives of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0003999303010281 -- used because the ABPMR-cited textbook (Grabois, et al. Physical Medicine: The Complete Approach. 2000, page 423) is not available to the user.",
