    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "Braddom's neurogenic bowel chapter (Ch. 21) notes that manual disimpaction and digital stimulation during bowel care are potent triggers of autonomic dysreflexia in patients with SCI at or above T6, and specifically recommends that 'if manual disimpaction is required, lubrication with lidocaine gel is recommended to decrease additional nociceptive sensory input from the richly innervated anal region,' i.e., use of a topical anesthetic to blunt the noxious afferent stimulus that provokes dysreflexia. Chemical stimulants such as bisacodyl or CO2-releasing suppositories can themselves precipitate or worsen dysreflexia rather than treat it, and neither addresses the immediate noxious rectal stimulation causing the episode. Applying a topical anesthetic before continuing digital stimulation is therefore the most appropriate next step.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 21, p.505. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 636.",
