    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "Mallet finger deformity results from rupture of the",
    "option_a": "extensor tendon insertion",
    "option_b": "profundus tendon",
    "option_c": "distal collateral ligaments",
    "option_d": "extensor indicis muscle",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "Braddom's hand splinting chapter (Ch. 11) describes the mallet finger injury as requiring 'a Stax splint, which is a static splint holding the DIP joint in full extension,' reflecting the underlying pathology of disruption of the terminal extensor tendon's insertion onto the distal phalanx, which produces a flexed DIP joint that cannot be actively extended. This is distinct from a flexor digitorum profundus rupture (which would impair DIP flexion, producing a 'jersey finger'), a collateral ligament injury (which causes lateral instability, not a flexion deformity), or extensor indicis dysfunction (which affects index finger MCP extension, not the DIP joint). The extension-blocking DIP splint used to treat mallet finger confirms that the extensor tendon insertion is the structure that has failed.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 11, p.274. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 836.",
