    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 24, Traumatic Brain Injury, primary injury section) states that cerebral contusions 'commonly occur at the crests of the gyri' and that 'contusions often occur on the undersurface of the frontal lobes, as well as the frontal and temporal tips, regardless of the site of impact, due to the internal architecture of the skull,' with an accompanying figure showing the anterotemporal and orbitofrontal regions as most frequently involved. Among the answer choices, the temporal lobe (specifically its anterior tip) is the structure most classically and most commonly contused in TBI due to its proximity to the bony sphenoid ridge, unlike the relatively protected occipital lobe, corpus callosum (a site of diffuse axonal injury rather than contusion), or midbrain.\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 24, p.578 (page differs from the original ABPMR citation of p.1680, 4th ed., which does not correspond to this topic in this 5th-ed. PDF). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 4th ed. 2005, page 1680.",
