    "explanation": "Braddom's traumatic brain injury chapter (Ch. 45) lists risk factors for the development of posttraumatic epilepsy (PTE), including 'biparietal contusions, dural penetration with bone and metal fragments, multiple intracranial operations, cortical contusions, subdural hematoma, significant midline shift, early [posttraumatic seizures], and skull fractures.' Dural penetration with bone or metal fragments -- i.e., a penetrating brain injury -- is explicitly called out among the strongest structural risk factors because it directly introduces cortical injury, hemorrhage, and epileptogenic scarring into brain tissue. Prolonged coma and diffuse hematomas (intracranial/subdural) correlate with overall injury severity but are not singled out as the leading risk factor for PTE the way penetrating/dural injury is.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 45, p.1068. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 1146.",
