    "explanation": "The ICF defines an 'impairment' as a problem in body function or structure (e.g., loss of muscle strength, loss of vision, or, as here, loss of hearing) -- essentially a deviation at the organ/system level. Occupational noise-induced hearing loss is precisely this: a documented structural/functional abnormality of the auditory system. In contrast, 'inability to walk one block' and 'inability to work due to chronic illness' are activity limitations and participation restrictions, respectively -- difficulties performing tasks or engaging in life roles that result from an underlying impairment, not the impairment itself. Short stature alone, absent an underlying pathological process, is typically a normal variant rather than a classified impairment.\nReference: PMC review, 'Measuring the ICF components of impairment, activity limitation and participation restriction,' https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2696439/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Barbotte et al., Bulletin of the World Health Organization, 2001, 79(11), page 1047) is not available to the user.",
