    "explanation": "This vignette (dashboard injury to a flexed knee, positive posterior drawer sign, difficulty walking down inclines) is the classic presentation of a posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) injury. Rehabilitation of PCL injuries emphasizes quadriceps strengthening because a strong quadriceps mechanism (via its pull on the tibial tubercle) resists posterior tibial translation and unloads the PCL, which is particularly important for tasks like descending inclines/stairs that stress the posterior-translated tibia; hamstring strengthening, by contrast, would tend to pull the tibia further posteriorly and can aggravate PCL laxity.\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010). Note: could not locate a directly supporting passage in the provided DeLisa PDF via keyword search (the knee-assessment chapter discusses PCL examination technique but the specific quadriceps-versus-hamstring rehabilitation principle for PCL injury was not found verbatim); explanation reflects standard PM&R/sports medicine teaching on this topic. Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 4th ed. 2005, page 572.",
