    "question_text": "A 52-year-old office worker presents with chronic headaches. She has failed numerous modalities and pharmacologic remedies. There is no history of substance abuse. Opioids were prescribed by her primary care doctor with success at aborting the headaches. She is concerned with the risk of addiction. You suggest that",
    "option_a": "she discontinue opioids since they are not indicated for headaches",
    "option_b": "she take naloxone as an antagonist to reduce the risk of addiction",
    "option_c": "addiction is highly unlikely if used for her medical treatment",
    "option_d": "she enter a drug treatment program to eliminate the opioids",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "Foundational pain-management literature (originating with Portenoy and Foley's observations on chronic non-cancer pain patients) and subsequent reviews indicate that when opioids are prescribed for legitimate medical treatment of pain and the patient has no personal history of substance-use disorder, the risk of developing true addiction is low, even though physical dependence and tolerance commonly develop and are not the same thing as addiction. A prior or current substance-use disorder is the major risk factor that substantially raises addiction risk on opioid exposure -- which this patient does not have. Given her documented benefit and absence of risk factors, abruptly discontinuing an effective, medically indicated treatment, using naloxone (an antagonist that would simply reverse analgesia, not treat 'addiction risk'), or referring her to a drug treatment program would be inappropriate and not supported by the evidence on opioid risk stratification.\nReference: PMC review, 'Long-term opioid management for chronic noncancer pain,' https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6494200/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Breitbart et al., Handbook of Pain Management, 2003, page 703) is not available to the user.",
