    "option_d": "Sural Please visit https://vimeo.com/140451103 to view the video.",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "This item is video-based, but Braddom's peripheral nerve/electrodiagnosis discussion of the ankle region (Ch. 44) describes the tibial nerve passing 'through the tarsal tunnel, which is formed by the flexor retinaculum that spans the space between the medial malleolus and the calcaneus,' before dividing into its terminal branches. A provocative test performed over this course posterior to the medial malleolus (e.g., percussion/compression, as in a Tinel sign at the tarsal tunnel) is designed to elicit paresthesias in the tibial nerve distribution, distinguishing it from the deep and superficial fibular (peroneal) nerves, which run on the dorsum/lateral leg, and the sural nerve, which is purely sensory and courses posterolateral to the ankle rather than through the tarsal tunnel.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 44, p.1028. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 1090.",
