    "option_d": "Rheumatoid arthritis",
    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity that classically presents in adolescents (typically 10-15 years old, often during growth spurts) as activity-related anterior knee pain localized directly over the tibial tubercle, frequently bilateral, as described in this vignette. Per pediatric orthopedic sources, it results from repetitive quadriceps traction on the immature tibial tubercle apophysis and is a clinical diagnosis based on this characteristic pain location and activity relationship. Patellar tendinitis and patellofemoral arthritis typically produce pain more diffusely around or behind the patella rather than at the tibial tubercle itself, and rheumatoid arthritis would be expected to show inflammatory signs (swelling, warmth) on exam rather than an unremarkable exam with isolated tubercle tenderness.\nReference: Scottish Rite for Children, overview of pediatric knee conditions/Osgood-Schlatter disease, https://scottishriteforchildren.org/spondylolysis-a-common-cause-of-back-pain-in-young-athletes/ (see also Medscape Osgood-Schlatter Disease overview, https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/411842-overview) -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (DeLee JC et al. DeLee & Drez's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine: Principles and Practice. 3rd ed. 2010, page 1527) is not available to the user.",
