    "id": "moc100-80",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A 57-year-old man sustained a stroke one year ago. He now has good voluntary control of the affected arm, with elbow and shoulder strength at 4/5, wrist extension at 2+/5, finger extension at 2-/5, and fair grip. Tone is minimally increased throughout the arm and sensation is intact. What is the most effective means of improving his hand function?",
    "option_a": "Restraining the unaffected hand during activities of daily living",
    "option_b": "Electromyographic biofeedback for the wrist extensors",
    "option_c": "Alternating hot and cold stimulation to the affected hand",
    "option_d": "Intensive training of the unaffected hand in one-handed techniques",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "This patient is an ideal candidate for constraint-induced movement therapy (CIMT): he has intact sensation, minimal tone increase, and enough residual voluntary movement (wrist/finger extension present, fair grip) in the affected hand -- the key inclusion criteria for CIMT protocols. CIMT works by restraining the less-affected (unaffected) hand, usually with a mitt or glove during waking hours, to force massed, repetitive practice of the affected limb, which reverses 'learned non-use' -- the phenomenon in which patients stop trying to use a weak but still-functional limb after early post-stroke experiences of poor performance. Randomized trials and Cochrane reviews summarized in the literature show CIMT produces meaningful, often durable improvements in affected-arm/hand function, whereas biofeedback alone, thermal stimulation, or training the unaffected hand in compensatory one-handed techniques do not address or actively reinforce non-use of the impaired hand.\nReference: PMC evidence-based analysis, 'Constraint-Induced Movement Therapy for Rehabilitation of Arm Dysfunction After Stroke in Adults,' https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3377570/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited original Liepert et al. Neuroscience Letters article (May 8, 1998) on motor cortex plasticity during CIMT, while foundational, is not available in full to the user; this accessible evidence review covering the same CIMT mechanism and outcomes was used instead.",
    "image": null,
    "video_link": ""
