    "id": "moc100-82",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A 17-year-old football player sustained a noncontact knee injury while planting his leg to make a cut. He heard a pop and felt his knee buckle. What is the most sensitive clinical test to establish the diagnosis?",
    "option_a": "Posterior drawer",
    "option_b": "McMurray",
    "option_c": "Lachman",
    "option_d": "Anterior drawer",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "The mechanism described -- a noncontact pivoting/cutting injury with an audible pop and the knee 'giving way' -- is the classic presentation of an anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tear. Meta-analyses of clinical ACL tests confirm the Lachman test is the most sensitive maneuver for diagnosing ACL rupture (pooled sensitivity around 85%, compared with lower sensitivity for the anterior drawer test), because it is performed near full extension/slight flexion where hamstring guarding is minimized and hemarthrosis interferes less than with drawer testing at 90 degrees. The posterior drawer test evaluates the PCL, and McMurray's test assesses for meniscal tears, so neither is the appropriate 'most sensitive' test for a suspected ACL injury.\nReference: StatPearls, 'Lachman Test,' NCBI Bookshelf, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK554415/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (DeLee JC et al. DeLee & Drez's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine: Principles and Practice. 3rd ed. 2010, page 1650) is not available to the user.",
