    "explanation": "The vertebral column is the most common skeletal site for bone metastases, reflecting its rich red marrow content and vascular supply via Batson's venous plexus, which allows hematogenous spread from pelvic and thoracic primary tumors without passing through the lungs first. Within the spine, reviews of spinal metastatic disease note roughly 70% of vertebral metastases occur in the thoracic spine, 20% lumbar, and 10% cervical, with the vertebral body itself (versus posterior elements) most frequently involved. Ribs, pelvis, and long bones are also common sites of metastatic disease but are each individually less frequently involved than the spine as a whole.\nReference: PMC review, 'Physiopathology of Spine Metastasis,' https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3265280/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Bos G, et al., Diagnosis and Management of Metastatic Bone Disease, 1987, page 221) is not available to the user.",
