    "id": "moc100-85",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "According to The Joint Commission patient safety guidelines for abbreviations, which order is written appropriately?",
    "option_a": "50 µ",
    "option_b": "5.0 µg",
    "option_c": "0.5 mg",
    "option_d": ".05 mg",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "The Joint Commission's 'Do Not Use' list (and its associated official 'Minimum List' of dangerous abbreviations, symbols, and dose designations) specifies that a trailing zero after a decimal (e.g., '5.0 mg') must never be used because a missed decimal point can cause a 10-fold overdose, and instead a value should be written without a trailing zero. It equally requires that a leading zero always precede a decimal fraction less than one (e.g., '0.5 mg', not '.5 mg'), for the same reason -- a missed decimal point otherwise causes a 10-fold error. Applying both rules: '0.5 mg' is correctly formatted, while '5.0 µg' violates the trailing-zero rule, '.05 mg' violates the leading-zero rule, and '50 µ' uses the dangerous, ambiguous 'µ' symbol on its own without a unit designation clearly spelled out.\nReference: Joint Commission 'Do Not Use' abbreviations list summary, https://www.beckersasc.com/asc-accreditation-and-patient-safety/the-joint-commission-eliminate-these-5-abbreviations/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited original source (The Joint Commission, Sentinel Event Alert, Issue 23, September 2001) is a primary Joint Commission bulletin not independently hosted for the user; this secondary summary of the same official policy was used instead.",
