    "id": "moc100-88",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "The long-term consequences of high bladder outlet resistance in children with myelomeningocele include",
    "option_a": "flaccid detrusor",
    "option_b": "retrograde ejaculation",
    "option_c": "ureteral dilatation",
    "option_d": "increased bladder compliance",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "In myelomeningocele, high bladder outlet resistance -- typically from detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia, present in roughly half of affected children -- creates a functional outlet obstruction analogous to an anatomic urethral obstruction. Urologic reviews describe that this chronically elevated storage/voiding pressure, especially when combined with reduced bladder compliance, places the upper urinary tract at significant risk for deterioration, manifesting as hydronephrosis, vesicoureteral reflux, and ureteral dilatation from the sustained back-pressure transmitted up the urinary tract. This is the opposite of a flaccid, low-pressure detrusor or increased compliance (which would be seen with lower, not higher, outlet resistance), and retrograde ejaculation is not a recognized consequence of pediatric bladder outlet resistance in this condition.\nReference: Translational Andrology and Urology review, 'Detrusor sphincter dyssynergia: a review of physiology, diagnosis, and treatment strategies,' https://tau.amegroups.org/article/view/8983/9709 -- used because the ABPMR-cited textbook (Kliegman, ed. Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics. 19th ed. 2011, page 2002) is not available to the user.",
