    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A 30-year-old man presents with low back, knee, and ankle pain of two months’ duration. He also has had burning on urination. Physical examination reveals a rash over the palms and soles. Sacroiliitis is present on radiographs. The clinical diagnosis is",
    "option_a": "ankylosing spondylitis",
    "option_b": "psoriatic arthropathy",
    "option_c": "gonococcal arthritis",
    "option_d": "reactive arthritis (Reiter disease)",
    "correct_option": "D",
    "explanation": "Braddom's spondyloarthropathy discussion (Ch. 33) lists reactive arthritis (Reiter syndrome) as a condition in which 'asymmetric sacroiliitis...usually begin[s] after a genitourinary or gastrointestinal infection,' with systemic symptoms and conjunctivitis common -- matching this patient's urethritis (burning on urination), lower-extremity/low back arthritis, and sacroiliitis. The classic triad also includes mucocutaneous findings such as keratoderma blennorrhagicum on the palms and soles, which corresponds to the described rash. Ankylosing spondylitis typically causes symmetric sacroiliitis without preceding GU/GI infection or a palmar/plantar rash; psoriatic arthropathy favors an oligoarticular distal joint pattern with psoriatic skin plaques rather than a preceding urethritis; and gonococcal arthritis causes a migratory tenosynovitis-arthritis-dermatitis syndrome without sacroiliitis as a defining feature.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 33, p.808. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 901.",
