    "id": "moc100-90",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR MOC100 (September 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "An expected motor conduction abnormality in severe radiculopathy is",
    "option_a": "prolonged distal latency",
    "option_b": "decreased amplitude",
    "option_c": "slowed velocity",
    "option_d": "abnormal temporal dispersion",
    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "In radiculopathy, motor conduction studies are usually normal because most muscles are innervated by more than one root, so a single-root lesion rarely knocks out enough motor axons to change the CMAP. However, when axon loss is severe enough (roughly 50% or more of the motor axons to a muscle), the reduced number of surviving axons produces a lower-amplitude CMAP when recording from a muscle predominantly supplied by the affected root -- this is the one motor NCS abnormality that can be seen in severe radiculopathy, in contrast to prolonged distal latency, slowed conduction velocity, or abnormal temporal dispersion, which reflect demyelinating/conduction-block pathophysiology not typical of root compression. Distal latency and conduction velocity remain normal because the demyelination in radiculopathy occurs proximal to the site tested and the surviving distal axons conduct normally.\nReference: StatPearls, 'Electrodiagnostic Evaluation of Lumbosacral Radiculopathy,' NCBI Bookshelf, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK563224/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Kimura J. Electrodiagnosis in Diseases of Nerve and Muscle. 4th ed. 2013, page 637) is not available to the user.",
