    "explanation": "Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, most commonly nifedipine, are the first-line pharmacologic agents for Raynaud phenomenon once nonpharmacologic measures (cold avoidance, avoiding vasoconstrictive triggers) are insufficient. A EULAR-referenced review and Medscape's treatment summary both identify CCBs, titrated to the maximum tolerated dose, as the drug class of choice before other vasodilators are added, whereas beta blockers are actually contraindicated because they promote peripheral vasoconstriction, and ACE inhibitors/ARBs lack the same evidence base for symptomatic vasospasm relief. A 2016 Cochrane review confirmed CCBs modestly but significantly reduce attack frequency compared with no pharmacologic therapy.\nReference: Medscape, 'Raynaud Phenomenon Treatment & Management,' https://emedicine.medscape.com/article/331197-treatment -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Herrick A. Current Opinion in Rheumatology. V23:6. 2011, pages 555-561) is not available to the user.",
