    "explanation": "A closed kinetic chain exercise is one in which the distal segment (the foot) is fixed against an immobile surface such as the ground, so a partial squat -- where the feet stay planted while the knees flex against body weight -- is a textbook closed-chain quadriceps exercise, engaging co-contraction of quadriceps, hamstrings, and hip/ankle stabilizers. In contrast, seated knee extension on a stacked-weight or isokinetic machine and an isometric quad set are open-chain movements because the distal segment (the lower leg/foot) moves freely and is not weight-bearing against a fixed surface. This distinction matters clinically because closed-chain exercises are generally favored in rehabilitation for their greater joint stability and functional carryover.\nReference: Wikipedia summary sourced from sports medicine texts, 'Closed kinetic chain exercises,' https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Closed_kinetic_chain_exercises -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (DeLee JC et al. DeLee & Drez's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine, 3rd ed. 2010, page 1668) is not available to the user.",
