    "option_d": "an ulnar to median nerve hand communication (Riche-Cannieu anastomosis)",
    "correct_option": "D",
    "explanation": "The Riche-Cannieu anastomosis is a communication in the palm between the deep (motor) branch of the ulnar nerve and the median nerve's recurrent thenar branch, allowing ulnar-nerve motor fibers to supply part or all of the thenar muscles including the opponens pollicis. This anatomic variant explains the case: despite a complete median nerve laceration at the wrist, residual (grade 3) thumb abduction persists because ulnar motor axons reaching the thenar muscles via this hand-level anastomosis are unaffected, while denervation changes (fibrillations, reduced recruitment) still appear in the muscle fibers that were solely median-innervated. This is distinguished from the Martin-Gruber anastomosis, which is a forearm-level median-to-ulnar communication and would not explain preserved thenar function after a wrist-level median laceration.\nReference: Silva et al., 'Riche-Cannieu Anastomosis: Structure, Function, and Clinical Significance,' Revista Brasileira de Ortopedia (PMC), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11193595/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Dumitru D et al. Electrodiagnostic Medicine, 2nd ed. 2002, page 192) is not available to the user.",
