    "id": "pmr100-12",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A 76-year-old woman is referred for rehabilitation following a recent left total hip arthroplasty. To prevent hip dislocation, you instruct the patient to avoid hip",
    "option_a": "adduction beyond neutral",
    "option_b": "abduction greater than 20 degrees",
    "option_c": "flexion greater than 45 degrees",
    "option_d": "external rotation beyond neutral",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "PM&R Secrets states that the direction of THA dislocation correlates with surgical approach, and that risk for posterior dislocation (the most common type, from the standard posterolateral approach) is with 'excessive flexion, adduction, and internal rotation,' while anterior dislocation risk is with 'hyperextension, adduction, and external rotation.' Notably, adduction beyond neutral appears in BOTH dislocation-risk directions, making it the single movement that must be avoided regardless of approach, whereas abduction is actually protective rather than a precipitant, and modest degrees of flexion or external rotation alone are safe. This is why standard hip precautions after THA universally instruct patients to avoid crossing the midline (adduction) in addition to avoiding excessive flexion/internal rotation or extension/external rotation depending on approach. Option B (abduction) is incorrect because abduction reduces dislocation risk rather than causing it.\nReference: O'Young et al. PM&R Secrets, 2nd ed. 2002 (THA dislocation risk factors and hip precautions), p.404. Original ABPMR answer key citation: O'Young et al. PM&R Secrets. 2nd ed. 2002, page 286.",
