    "option_d": "increase the base of support, thus increasing stability and balance",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "Braddom's chapter on assistive devices explains that during single-limb stance the hip joint must counteract a load of roughly four times body weight, generated by gravity plus the pull of the gluteus medius-minimus complex needed to keep the pelvis level. A cane held in the hand contralateral to the arthritic hip is advanced with the affected limb; the upper-limb force applied through the cane substitutes for part of the abductor moment, directly decreasing the compressive/weight-bearing force across the diseased hip joint. This is why canes are prescribed for painful hip arthritis rather than simply for balance (choice D) or sensory scanning (choice B), and the cane compensates for the biomechanics of gluteus medius action rather than the muscle weakness itself (choice C).\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 9, p.270. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 845.",
