    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "What is the most common mechanism for Achilles tendon rupture?",
    "option_a": "Eccentric dorsiflexion",
    "option_b": "Eccentric plantar flexion",
    "option_c": "Passive dorsiflexion",
    "option_d": "Passive plantar flexion",
    "correct_option": "A",
    "explanation": "Achilles tendon rupture most commonly occurs when the calf muscle-tendon unit is forcibly loaded eccentrically -- the gastrocnemius/soleus complex contracts to resist dorsiflexion (as in a sudden push-off or unexpected forward lunge) while the tendon is simultaneously being lengthened by ankle dorsiflexion. Orthopedic sources note that most ruptures (roughly half) occur during forceful weight-bearing push-off with the knee extended, such as sprinting or jumping, and emphasize that the injury requires active, resisted muscle contraction during dorsiflexion -- passive dorsiflexion with a relaxed muscle does not tear the tendon, which rules out the 'passive' distractor options. This mechanism (eccentric loading during dorsiflexion) is why the injury is common in 'weekend warrior' athletes performing sudden explosive movements.\nReference: OrthoPaedia, 'Achilles Tendon Rupture,' https://www.orthopaedia.com/achilles-tendon-rupture/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Pedowitz D, Kirwan G. Achilles tendon ruptures. Curr Rev Musculoskeletal Med. 2013;6(4):285-293) could not be directly retrieved in full text.",
