    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "During nerve conduction studies (NCSs), what is defined as the maximal stimulus?",
    "option_a": "The highest intensity of stimulus that the patient can tolerate",
    "option_b": "Stimulus beyond which there is no further increase in the evoked amplitude",
    "option_c": "Stimulus about 20-30% greater than the threshold stimulation",
    "option_d": "Any stimulus which is greater than the threshold stimulus",
    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "In nerve conduction studies, as stimulus intensity is increased from threshold, the CMAP/SNAP amplitude grows as progressively more axons are recruited; the maximal stimulus is defined as the point at which all axons in the nerve have been depolarized, so that further increases in current produce no further increase in the evoked response amplitude. A supramaximal stimulus is then applied by increasing the intensity an additional 10-20% beyond this maximal point to ensure all fibers are consistently activated despite minor technical variability -- meaning options describing a fixed percentage above threshold, patient tolerance, or 'any' stimulus above threshold are all incorrect definitions of 'maximal.'\nReference: PM&R KnowledgeNow (AAPM&R), 'Nerve Conduction Studies,' https://now.aapmr.org/nerve-conduction-velocities/; and Vinik AI et al., 'Supramaximal Stimulus Intensity as a Diagnostic Tool in Chronic Demyelinating Neuropathy,' PMC, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4927946/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Kimura J. Electrodiagnosis in Diseases of Nerve and Muscle, 4th ed. 2013, page 76) is not available to the user.",
