    "explanation": "Braddom's traumatic brain injury chapter describes the secondary injury cascade following TBI: excess excitatory amino acid (glutamate) release triggers calcium influx, which drives 'EAA-mediated Ca++-dependent production of nitric oxide, superoxide, and free radical damage to DNA and cellular membranes leading to cell death.' Free-reactive iron released from hemorrhage further catalyzes free radical formation, and superoxide radicals are described as 'a principal mediator of the microvascular damage after TBI.' This oxidative/free-radical mechanism—not changes in extracellular magnesium, calcium, or potassium concentration per se—is highlighted as a key contributor to secondary neuronal injury.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 43, p.965. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 1136.",
