    "explanation": "In an elderly patient, advanced spondylotic changes are essentially universal on imaging and often anatomically nonspecific -- structural studies like CT, MRI, or myelography can show foraminal narrowing or disc disease at multiple levels but cannot establish which, if any, level is physiologically producing the patient's radicular symptoms. Electrodiagnostic testing (NCS/EMG), in contrast, directly assesses whether a specific nerve root is functionally denervated (e.g., via fibrillations and reduced recruitment in a myotomal distribution), making it the study best suited to confirm actual neurologic dysfunction and correlate the patient's radicular pain with a physiologically active root lesion rather than incidental degenerative findings. PM&R KnowledgeNow's review of radiculopathy electrodiagnosis explains that EMG is particularly useful for confirming nerve root dysfunction when structural imaging findings are equivocal or nonspecific relative to clinical findings, as is common with age-related spondylosis.\nReference: PM&R KnowledgeNow (AAPM&R), 'Electrodiagnosis of Radiculopathies (Cervical, Thoracic, and Lumbar),' https://now.aapmr.org/electrodiagnosis-of-radiculopathies-cervical-thoracic-and-lumbar/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Dumitru D et al. Electrodiagnostic Medicine, 2nd ed. 2002, page 420) is not available to the user.",
