    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "In patients with a spinal cord injury (SCI), which type of pain is most responsive to transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)?",
    "option_a": "Deafferentation central",
    "option_b": "Radicular",
    "option_c": "Complex regional pain syndrome",
    "option_d": "Visceral",
    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "The pain chapter groups TENS among modalities with mechanisms directed at peripheral/segmental nociceptive and proprioceptive afferent modulation in the dorsal horn, describing how TENS 'may influence spinal sensory processing with reduced activity in those afferents or with increased inhibition at dorsal horn neurons,' a mechanism that is best suited to pain arising from an identifiable peripheral/nerve root (radicular) source where afferent gating can meaningfully reduce transmission. In contrast, central deafferentation pain arises from injury to the spinal cord pathways themselves (above the level of peripheral nerve input TENS can modulate), CRPS involves complex central and autonomic dysregulation less amenable to simple afferent gating, and visceral pain is mediated by different, less segmentally organized afferent pathways — none of which respond as reliably to TENS as radicular (nerve root) pain does.\nReference: Lin VW et al. Spinal Cord Medicine: Principles and Practice, 2nd ed., p.2106 (TENS mechanism discussion, pain chapter). Original ABPMR answer key citation: Lin VW et al. Spinal Cord Medicine: Principles and Practice. 2nd ed. 2010, page 517.",
