    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 50, 'Secondary Conditions and Complications', spasticity pharmacotherapy section) states: 'The benzodiazepines' mechanism of action is central in origin, acting on the brainstem reticular formation and spinal polysynaptic pathways. The benzodiazepines demonstrate their effect via GABAA (g-aminobutyric acid), which opens membrane Cl- channels with resultant hyperpolarization.' It further notes that 'the greatest benefit for diazepam has been demonstrated in the SCI population.' This confirms that diazepam's antispasmodic action in SCI is mediated primarily through GABA-A receptors, not GABA-B (the target of baclofen) or alpha-adrenergic receptors (targets of tizanidine and clonidine).\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 50, p.1326 (page number confirmed matching the original ABPMR citation exactly). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 5th ed. 2010, page 1326.",
