    "id": "pmr100-67",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "Pharmacologic strategies employed to improve arousal for patients in a minimally conscious/vegetative state after severe traumatic brain injury (TBI) would most likely include stimulation of",
    "option_a": "glutamate receptors",
    "option_b": "gamma-aminobutyric acid inhibition pathways",
    "option_c": "serotonergic pathways",
    "option_d": "dopaminergic pathways",
    "correct_option": "D",
    "explanation": "The Traumatic Brain Injury text describes dopamine (from the ventral tegmental area, projecting primarily to prefrontal cortex) as a key neuromodulator targeted pharmacologically to improve arousal and cognitive functioning after TBI, and notes that dopaminergic agents (e.g., amantadine, a dopamine reuptake inhibitor/agonist) are used to support recovery of consciousness. In contrast, the text states that GABA agonists such as benzodiazepines and baclofen \"may reduce the rate of recovery from TBI\" and should be minimized, and that dopamine antagonists like haloperidol \"impede learning and recovery\" -- meaning arousal-promoting strategies target dopaminergic enhancement rather than GABAergic or antidopaminergic pathways. This supports stimulation of dopaminergic pathways as the pharmacologic mechanism used to improve arousal in minimally conscious/vegetative TBI patients, rather than glutamate or serotonin pathways, which are not highlighted as primary arousal-promoting targets in this text.\nReference: Dopaminergic pharmacotherapy for arousal after TBI as discussed in Traumatic Brain Injury (cognitive rehabilitation/pharmacotherapy chapter), pp.342-343 (used as a topical secondary source; the original ABPMR-cited textbook, Zasler ND et al. Brain Injury Medicine, 2nd ed. 2013, was not available to the user). Original ABPMR answer key citation: Zasler ND et al. Brain Injury Medicine. 2nd ed. 2013, page 525.",
