    "id": "pmr100-70",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "The following tracing represents A video file accompanies this item. On the computer-based exam, a video would be automatically displayed here.",
    "option_a": "myokymia",
    "option_b": "myotonia Please visit",
    "option_c": "complex repetitive discharge https://vimeo.com/131888180",
    "option_d": "endplate potentials to view the video.",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "This item is video-based (the original text is corrupted by the video-link formatting), and the correct answer is complex repetitive discharge (CRD). CRDs are a form of pathologic spontaneous EMG activity produced by a group of adjacent muscle fibers discharging synchronously in an ephaptic, circuitous pattern; on the loudspeaker and oscilloscope they have a very distinctive sound and appearance -- trains of complex polyphasic (or serrated) potentials firing at a stable, regular rate (roughly 5-100 Hz) that begin and end abruptly, sounding like a \"machine gun.\" This waveform is easily distinguished visually/audibly from myokymic discharges (grouped, bursting doublets/triplets with pauses), myotonic discharges (waxing-and-waning \"dive bomber\" sound), and endplate potentials/spikes (irregular, brief, seen only at the endplate zone). CRDs are seen in chronic neurogenic or myopathic conditions with long-standing denervation and reinnervation.\nReference: Gupta N, et al. Incidental complex repetitive discharges on needle electromyography. Muscle Nerve. 2023 (summarized), https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36416248/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Kimura J. Electrodiagnosis in Diseases of Nerve and Muscle, 4th ed., 2013, page 372) is not available to the user.",
    "image": null,
