    "id": "pmr100-75",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A middle-aged man who fell on his outstretched arm has pain in his shoulder and is unable to initiate abduction of the arm. Examination reveals weakness of shoulder abduction and external rotation and tenderness under the acromion. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
    "option_a": "Partial C5 root avulsion",
    "option_b": "Clavicular fracture",
    "option_c": "Rotator cuff tear",
    "option_d": "Suprascapular nerve injury",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 35, 'Upper Extremity Soft-Tissue Injuries', rotator cuff section) describes the classic mechanism and presentation of rotator cuff tear, including pain 'at the site along the tuberosity...pain along the lateral aspect of the arm toward the insertion of the deltoid, and pain with overhead activities...a drop arm test...noted' and weakness on manual strength testing of the supraspinatus and external rotators. A fall on an outstretched arm producing weakness of abduction and external rotation with subacromial tenderness and inability to initiate abduction is the classic presentation of an acute traumatic rotator cuff tear, as opposed to a C5 root avulsion (which would show more diffuse myotomal weakness and sensory loss), clavicular fracture (bony tenderness and deformity, not primarily weakness), or isolated suprascapular nerve injury (weakness limited to supraspinatus/infraspinatus without the described exam pattern).\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 35, p.913 (page number confirmed matching the original ABPMR citation exactly). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 5th ed. 2010, page 913.",
