    "id": "pmr100-76",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "Which recreational activity is contraindicated following a total knee arthroplasty?",
    "option_a": "Hiking",
    "option_b": "Power walking",
    "option_c": "Jogging",
    "option_d": "Cycling",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "Standard orthopedic guidance after total knee arthroplasty (TKA) divides activities into recommended low-impact options and discouraged high-impact options based on the repetitive impact loading they place on the prosthetic components. The AAOS patient guidance on activities after knee replacement lists low-impact activities such as walking, hiking, stationary or road cycling, swimming, and golf as generally encouraged, while high-impact activities that involve running, jumping, or repetitive pounding -- including jogging/running -- are discouraged because they accelerate polyethylene wear and increase the risk of implant loosening or failure. Hiking, power walking, and cycling are all classified as lower-impact activities appropriate after TKA, whereas jogging is the classic high-impact activity that remains contraindicated in standard post-arthroplasty activity counseling.\nReference: American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons (AAOS), \"Activities After Total Knee Replacement,\" OrthoInfo, https://orthoinfo.aaos.org/en/recovery/activities-after-knee-replacement/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (DeLee JC et al. DeLee & Drez's Orthopaedic Sports Medicine, 3rd ed., 2010, page 1789) is not available to the user.",
