    "id": "pmr100-77",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "Which class of antidepressant medication has a narrow therapeutic index and is associated with fatal heart block or ventricular arrythmias at toxic doses?",
    "option_a": "Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors",
    "option_b": "Serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors",
    "option_c": "Tricyclic antidepressants",
    "option_d": "Monoamine oxidase inhibitors",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) have a notably narrow therapeutic index; toxic ingestions of only a few times the therapeutic dose (often less than a week's supply) can be lethal. TCA cardiotoxicity is driven by cardiac fast sodium-channel blockade, which prolongs the QRS duration, delays atrioventricular conduction (producing heart block), and predisposes to ventricular tachyarrhythmias -- with QRS prolongation beyond 160 ms being predictive of ventricular arrhythmia. This cardiotoxic profile is unique among the listed classes: SSRIs and SNRIs are comparatively far safer in overdose, and MAOIs, while dangerous via hypertensive crisis/serotonin syndrome, are not classically defined by heart-block/ventricular-arrhythmia toxicity from sodium channel blockade the way TCAs are.\nReference: StatPearls - Tricyclic Antidepressant Toxicity, NCBI Bookshelf, https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430931/ -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Janicak PG et al. Principles and Practice of Psychopharmacotherapy, 4th ed., 2006, page 269) is not available to the user.",
