    "id": "pmr100-78",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A patient on methadone presented with euphoria, agitation, and pupillary constriction. He subsequently had progressive drowsiness leading into a coma. He most likely has opioid- related",
    "option_a": "allergic reaction",
    "option_b": "psychotic disorder",
    "option_c": "withdrawal",
    "option_d": "intoxication",
    "correct_option": "D",
    "explanation": "Acute opioid intoxication/overdose is characterized by the classic triad of CNS depression (progressing from drowsiness to stupor to coma), miosis (pupillary constriction), and respiratory depression, sometimes preceded by an initial euphoric or agitated phase as the drug takes effect. This time course -- euphoria/agitation followed by progressively worsening sedation into coma, with constricted pupils -- is the hallmark of opioid (including methadone) intoxication, not withdrawal, which instead produces mydriasis, piloerection, diarrhea, and autonomic hyperactivity without CNS depression or coma. An allergic reaction or psychotic disorder would not explain the progressive drowsiness-to-coma trajectory or miosis.\nReference: MSD Manual Professional Edition, \"Opioid Toxicity and Withdrawal,\" https://www.msdmanuals.com/professional/psychiatric-disorders/opioid-use-disorder/opioid-toxicity-and-withdrawal -- used because the ABPMR-cited source (Medline Plus, www.nlm.nih.gov) could not be retrieved verbatim, though it matches the topic cited by ABPMR.",
