    "id": "pmr100-80",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "A patient who sustained a stroke presents with spasticity in the wrist flexors which has been treated with botulinum toxin. Which modality will be most effective when applied to the wrist extensors?",
    "option_a": "Massage",
    "option_b": "Ultrasound",
    "option_c": "Muscle cooling",
    "option_d": "Electrical stimulation",
    "correct_option": "D",
    "explanation": "When one muscle group (wrist flexors) has been chemodenervated with botulinum toxin for spasticity, electrical stimulation applied to the antagonist (wrist extensors) is used to promote active extensor recruitment and reciprocal inhibition of the now-weakened flexor tone, functionally rebalancing the wrist and reinforcing extensor strengthening during the toxin's window of action; this differs from ultrasound, massage, and cooling, which are used for local soft-tissue or pain effects rather than promoting active antagonist muscle activity after chemodenervation.\nReference: Note: could not locate a directly supporting passage in the provided Braddom 5th ed. PDF via keyword search; explanation reflects standard PM&R teaching on combining botulinum toxin with electrical stimulation of the antagonist muscle. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 653.",
