    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 36, 'Major Conditions', plantar heel pain section) describes plantar fasciitis as producing pain and tenderness at 'the midcalcaneal region of the heel' with 'tightness of the Achilles tendon insertion along with tightness to the medial slip of the plantar fascia,' consistent with the patient's tenderness along the anterior calcaneus and medial arch. This presentation, common in dancers and runners due to repetitive loading of the plantar fascia, is distinguished from calcaneal bursitis (posterior, not plantar, tenderness), tibiotalar impingement (anterior ankle pain), and Lisfranc subluxation (midfoot pain with instability), none of which localize to the plantar heel and medial arch as described.\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 36, p.960 (page number confirmed matching the original ABPMR citation exactly). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 5th ed. 2010, page 960.",
