    "id": "pmr100-96",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "In a patient who sustained a thalamic stroke, how are pain and temperature perception affected below the level of the lesion? Location Temperature",
    "option_a": "Ipsilateral Loss",
    "option_b": "Contralateral Loss",
    "option_c": "Ipsilateral Intact",
    "option_d": "Contralateral Intact",
    "correct_option": "B",
    "explanation": "DeLisa's Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice (Ch. 49, 'Treatment of the Patient with Chronic Pain', 'Poststroke Pain' section) states: 'Central pain that is due to thalamic infarction is often characterized as an agonizing, burning pain on the side contralateral to that of the lesion...Almost 100% of central pain patients are affected by change in temperature perception.' This confirms that after a thalamic stroke, both pain and temperature sensation are lost (or abnormally perceived) on the side of the body contralateral to the lesion, consistent with the crossed nature of the spinothalamic pathway relaying these modalities through the thalamus.\nReference: DeLisa JA, ed., et al. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation Medicine: Principles and Practice, 5th ed. (2010), Ch. 49, p.1307 (page number confirmed matching the original ABPMR citation exactly). Original ABPMR answer key citation: DeLisa 5th ed. 2010, page 1307.",
