    "id": "pmr100-98",
    "source_exam": "ABPMR PM&R100 Part I (June 2015 release)",
    "question_text": "When combined with active range of motion (ROM) exercise, which modality can improve hand function in individuals with scleroderma (systemic sclerosis) or rheumatoid arthritis (RA)?",
    "option_a": "Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation",
    "option_b": "Therapeutic ultrasound",
    "option_c": "Cryotherapy",
    "option_d": "Paraffin baths",
    "correct_option": "D",
    "explanation": "Braddom's physical agent modalities chapter describes the paraffin bath as 'a simple and efficient method to apply superficial heat, especially to the small joints of the body such as the interphalangeal joints,' noting that it 'is most commonly used in rheumatoid arthritis and osteoarthritis,' and pairs it with active range-of-motion exercise (illustrated in the text's video on 'Paraffin Bath for Rheumatoid Hands'). Its combination of low specific heat with high total heat content lets patients tolerate warming of stiff, small hand joints better than water immersion, making it especially suited to improving hand mobility in scleroderma or RA when combined with active ROM, compared with TENS, ultrasound, or cryotherapy, which do not provide the same deep, well-tolerated heating of multiple small joints.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 17, p.373. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 453.",
