    "explanation": "Braddom's chapter on disorders affecting the neck/spine lists diskitis among infectious causes of spinal pain and notes that it 'can occur from contiguous spread of infection or iatrogenically from procedures such as diskectomy and diskography,' typically presenting weeks after the index procedure with progressive, unrelenting pain rather than the radicular pattern of the original disk herniation. Gadolinium-enhanced MRI is the imaging study of choice in this setting because contrast enhancement helps distinguish postoperative infection (diskitis/vertebral osteomyelitis) from expected postsurgical scar or recurrent disk material, which plain radiographs, ultrasound, or a bone scan cannot reliably differentiate.\nReference: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, 5th ed. (2016), Ch. 36, p.738. Original ABPMR answer key citation: Braddom RL, ed. Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation. 4th ed. 2011, page 143.",
