    "option_c": "Consultation for carotid endarterectomy",
    "option_d": "Begin warfarin for INR goal 2–3",
    "correct_option": "C",
    "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The patient demonstrates transient ischemic attacks attributable to a symptomatic left internal carotid artery stenosis with acute stroke in the anterior circulation, watershed infarct. Though cardiac etiology is still possible the history also of amaurosis fugax in the left eye supports extracranial artery symptomatic stenosis as the source. Revascularization with carotid endarterectomy (CEA) is preferred within 2 weeks of the ischemic event, rather than delaying intervention. CEA or stenting is contraindicated in those with 100% or complete occlusion of internal carotid artery. Cardiac catheterization might be necessary for assessing pre-operative risk but is not the immediate next step in stroke prevention. There is no advantage in oral anticoagulation over antiplatelets in the secondary prevention of ischemic stroke in non-cardioembolic strokes (WARSS-Warfarin-Aspirin Recurrent Stroke Study). (Yavagal and Haussen 2011).",
