    "explanation": "Answer D. All of the above testing can be used in determination of brain death. AAN guidelines advise that ancillary tests be used when uncertainty exists about parts of the neurologic exam or when the apnea test cannot be performed. These tests do not replace the clinical examination. EEG testing must show 30 minutes of electrocerebral silence in brain death. Nuclear cerebral blood flow testing or SPECT must demonstrate complete absence of cerebral perfusion in brain death. Catheter angiography shows absent intracranial blood flow above the level of the proximal internal carotid and vertebral arteries in brain death (Webb and Samuels 2012).",
