    "question_text": "A 75 year-old man with 10 years of progressive Parkinson’s disease begins to have recurrent falls at home. His family becomes worried about signs of “dementia” presenting over one month. He is more forgetful, not talking as much, and increasingly taking naps during the day. His family takes him to the doctor’s office. A change in his mental status is seen. He is drowsy, disoriented, inattentive and with mild right hemiparesis. A CT head is ordered, so what is the most likely finding?",
