    "question_text": "A 42 year-old man, accountant, with chronic alcoholism presents to the ­emergency room because of blurred vision and falls increasing over the last 3 weeks. He admits to recently losing his job and having increasing consumption of alcohol in this period. On exam he is disoriented to time and place, with marked short-term memory impairment, has gross dysconjugate gaze, nystagmus in all extremes of gaze, and an inability to move his eyes fully up or down. He has a very wide based gait, and is unable to tandem walk. On MRI brain he is found to have bilateral thalamic hyperintensities. What is the most urgent treatment needed to reverse symptoms and prevent progression?",
